The proof has been written to `proof.md`.

**Summary**: The answer is **No** in general. The counterexample is the open immersion $\mathrm{Spec}\,k[x,x^{-1}] \to \mathrm{Spec}\,k[x]$ with $a = x^{-1}$: it is flat (localization), has reduced fibers (the fiber over the origin is empty, all others are reduced points or the generic field), and $x^{-1}$ is constant on every fiber — yet $x^{-1} \notin k[x]$. The mechanism is that the missing (empty) fiber over the origin leaves no constraint forcing $x^{-1}$ to extend, and the cokernel $M = k[x,x^{-1}]/k[x]$ is a nonzero module that vanishes in every fiber (impossible for f.g. modules, but $M$ is not finitely generated). The faithfully flat (surjective) case is also sketched: there the answer becomes Yes via a Tor argument showing $B = A \cap \mathrm{Frac}(B)$.
